::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 3x + 5x. {
=A. 8x#Correct. Both terms are x-terms, so 3x + 5x = 8x.
~B. 15x#This multiplies the coefficients instead of adding them. Like terms are combined by adding coefficients.
~C. 8x<sup>2</sup>#This changes the variable part. Combining like terms keeps the same variable part.
~D. 3x + 5#This treats 5x as if it were 5. Both terms include x.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 9a - 4a. {
~A. 13a#This adds 9a and 4a instead of subtracting. The operation sign matters.
~B. 5a<sup>2</sup>#This changes the variable part. Combining like terms keeps the variable a.
=C. 5a#Correct. 9a - 4a = 5a.
~D. 36a#This multiplies the coefficients. Like terms are combined by addition or subtraction.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 6m + 2 + 3m. {
~A. 11m#This combines the constant with the variable terms. Constants are not like terms with m-terms.
=B. 9m + 2#Correct. Combine 6m and 3m, and keep the constant 2 separate.
~C. 9m#This combines the m-terms but leaves out the constant.
~D. 6m + 5#This combines 2 and 3m incorrectly. A constant and a variable term are not like terms.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 7p - 2p + 8. {
~A. 9p + 8#This adds 7p and 2p instead of subtracting 2p.
~B. 5p#This combines the p-terms but omits the constant 8.
~C. 13p#This combines all numbers as if they were coefficients of p. The 8 is a constant.
=D. 5p + 8#Correct. 7p - 2p = 5p, and the constant 8 remains.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 4x + 3 + 2x + 5. {
=A. 6x + 8#Correct. Combine 4x and 2x to get 6x, and combine 3 and 5 to get 8.
~B. 6x + 3#This combines the x-terms but leaves out the constant 5.
~C. 14x#This combines constants with variable terms. Constants and x-terms are not like terms.
~D. 9x + 5#This combines 4x and 5 incorrectly. Only like terms can be combined.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 10n - 3n - 4. {
~A. 13n - 4#This adds 10n and 3n instead of subtracting 3n.
=B. 7n - 4#Correct. 10n - 3n = 7n, and -4 remains.
~C. 7n#This leaves out the constant -4.
~D. 3n#This subtracts the constant from the coefficient. Constants do not combine with n-terms.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite 2(x + 6) using the distributive property. {
~A. 2x + 6#This does not multiply 2 by 6. Distribute to every term.
~B. 8x#This adds 2 and 6 and attaches x. Distribution uses multiplication.
~C. 12x#This multiplies 2 and 6 but does not include the x-term correctly.
=D. 2x + 12#Correct. 2 times x is 2x, and 2 times 6 is 12.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite 5(a + 3) using the distributive property. {
~A. 5a + 3#This multiplies 5 by a but not by 3.
~B. 8a#This adds 5 and 3 and attaches a. Distribution requires multiplication.
~C. 15a#This multiplies 5 and 3 but does not include the a-term correctly.
=D. 5a + 15#Correct. Multiply 5 by a and multiply 5 by 3.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite 3(y - 4) using the distributive property. {
=A. 3y - 12#Correct. Multiply 3 by y and multiply 3 by -4.
~B. 3y - 4#This does not multiply 3 by -4. Distribute to every term.
~C. 3y + 12#This changes the sign. 3 times -4 is -12.
~D. 7y#This adds 3 and 4 and ignores the subtraction structure.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite 4(2b + 1) using the distributive property. {
~A. 8b + 1#This distributes 4 to 2b but not to 1.
~B. 6b + 1#This adds 4 and 2b instead of multiplying.
~C. 8b#This leaves out the constant term created by distribution.
=D. 8b + 4#Correct. 4 times 2b is 8b, and 4 times 1 is 4.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite 6(3m - 2) using the distributive property. {
~A. 18m - 2#This does not multiply 6 by -2.
=B. 18m - 12#Correct. 6 times 3m is 18m, and 6 times -2 is -12.
~C. 9m - 12#This adds 6 and 3m instead of multiplying.
~D. 18m + 12#This changes the sign of the second term.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite -2(x + 5) using the distributive property. {
~A. -2x + 5#This does not multiply -2 by 5.
~B. 2x + 10#This ignores the negative sign on -2.
=C. -2x - 10#Correct. -2 times x is -2x, and -2 times 5 is -10.
~D. -7x#This combines -2 and 5 as if they were like terms.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 3(x + 2) + 4x. {
=A. 7x + 6#Correct. 3(x + 2) = 3x + 6, and 3x + 4x = 7x.
~B. 3x + 6#This distributes correctly but does not combine the additional 4x.
~C. 7x + 2#This combines x-terms but does not distribute 3 to 2.
~D. 9x#This combines numbers and variables incorrectly. Constants stay separate unless they have like terms.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 2(a + 5) + 3a. {
~A. 2a + 10#This distributes correctly but does not combine the 3a term.
~B. 5a + 5#This does not multiply 2 by 5.
=C. 5a + 10#Correct. 2(a + 5) = 2a + 10, and 2a + 3a = 5a.
~D. 10a#This combines the constant with variable terms. Constants are not like terms with a-terms.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 5(n - 1) + 2n. {
~A. 5n - 1#This does not multiply 5 by -1 and does not combine 2n.
~B. 7n + 5#This uses the wrong sign for the constant term.
~C. 3n - 5#This subtracts the n-terms instead of adding 5n and 2n.
=D. 7n - 5#Correct. 5(n - 1) = 5n - 5, and 5n + 2n = 7n.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 4(2p + 3) - p. {
~A. 8p + 3#This does not multiply 4 by 3 and does not subtract p.
=B. 7p + 12#Correct. 4(2p + 3) = 8p + 12, and 8p - p = 7p.
~C. 8p + 12#This distributes correctly but does not combine -p.
~D. 9p + 12#This adds p instead of subtracting p.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to 6x + 9x? {
=A. 15x#Correct. 6x + 9x = 15x.
~B. 54x#This multiplies the coefficients. Like terms are combined by adding coefficients.
~C. 15x<sup>2</sup>#This changes the variable part. The variable remains x.
~D. 6x + 9#This changes 9x into a constant.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to 3(2x + 4)? {
~A. 6x + 4#This distributes to 2x but not to 4.
=B. 6x + 12#Correct. Distribute 3 to both 2x and 4.
~C. 5x + 7#This adds terms instead of multiplying by the outside factor.
~D. 6x#This leaves out the constant term created by distribution.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair of terms are like terms in 5x + 2 + 3x? {
~A. 5x and 2#A variable term and a constant are not like terms.
~B. 2 and 3x#A constant and a variable term are not like terms.
=C. 5x and 3x#Correct. Both terms have the same variable part, x.
~D. 5x and 3#There is no constant 3 in the expression; 3 is the coefficient of 3x.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Why can 4x + 3 not be simplified by combining terms? {
~A. Because 4x is already a constant#4x is a variable term, not a constant.
~B. Because addition cannot be used in expressions#Addition can be used; the issue is that the terms are not like terms.
=C. Because 4x and 3 are not like terms#Correct. 4x has a variable, while 3 is a constant.
~D. Because x must always equal 3#The variable x is not assigned a value here. The expression is being simplified symbolically.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student wrote 2(x + 7) = 2x + 7. What is the correct expression? {
=A. 2x + 14#Correct. 2 times x is 2x, and 2 times 7 is 14.
~B. 2x + 7#This repeats the mistake. The 2 must also multiply 7.
~C. 9x#This adds 2 and 7 and attaches x, which is not distribution.
~D. 14x#This multiplies 2 and 7 but leaves out the x-term structure.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student wrote 5a + 3a = 8a<sup>2</sup>. What is the correct simplification? {
~A. 15a#This multiplies the coefficients. Like terms are combined by adding coefficients.
~B. 8a<sup>2</sup>#This is the original mistake. Adding like terms does not change a into a squared term.
=C. 8a#Correct. Add the coefficients and keep the variable part a.
~D. 2a#This subtracts the coefficients instead of adding them.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify x + x + x. {
~A. x<sup>3</sup>#This treats repeated addition as an exponent. x + x + x means 3 copies of x.
=B. 3x#Correct. Three x-terms combine to make 3x.
~C. x + 3#This changes two x-terms into a constant. All three terms are x-terms.
~D. 1x#This counts only one x-term instead of all three.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 8 - 3x + 5x. {
~A. 10x#This combines the constant with the variable terms. The 8 is not an x-term.
~B. 8 + 8x#This adds the coefficients incorrectly. -3x + 5x = 2x.
~C. -8x + 8#This subtracts the coefficients in the wrong order.
=D. 2x + 8#Correct. Combine -3x and 5x to get 2x, and keep the constant 8.




}

::ALG1_U01_L07_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_L07_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to 2(3x + 1) + x? {
=A. 7x + 2#Correct. 2(3x + 1) = 6x + 2, and 6x + x = 7x.
~B. 6x + 1#This does not multiply 2 by 1 and does not combine the extra x.
~C. 6x + 2#This distributes correctly but does not combine the extra x.
~D. 8x + 2#This combines 6x and x as if x had coefficient 2. The coefficient of x is 1.




}
